@toc

# How to Use These Answers

## What is in this document

@py qa_index 6

## How every answer is built

1. **The question**, exactly as set, with its year and number.
2. **The answer map** — a mind map whose centre states what the question asks, part by part. Every branch is one numbered heading of the answer, and every point on it is a short phrase that makes complete sense on its own — so the map alone tells the examiner the whole argument. Draw a quick version in the margin of your answer book before you write.
3. **Decoding the question** — the command words and what each part demands.
4. **The model answer** — 1,000 to 1,300 words (550 to 650 for a short note), the length a strong candidate can write in about thirty minutes. It is built in two levels: main headings **1, 2, 3…** taken from the question's own words, and sub-headings **i), ii), iii)…** under them. The introduction always has a simple **definition** and the **argument**. Each sub-heading answers one point in a short paragraph with the **key words in bold**. Every answer has at least one **figure** besides the answer map (a comparison, flow, cycle, pyramid, grid, timeline or mapping), a Pakistan angle and a balanced conclusion.
5. **Why this answer scores** — what the examiner rewards in it.

::: eye | Topic 6 is a topic of judgements
Almost every question here asks you to **judge**: pros and cons (2016), "comment" (2017), "do you think" (2018), "useful or preferential" (2020), "is it showing results?" (2021), "in your opinion" (2022), "is it a human right?" (2024). Notice how the same core material is re-cut each time — **Pitkin** (descriptive vs substantive representation), **Dahlerup** (fast track, critical mass), the **quota history** (1956, 1962, 1973, 1985, the lapse, 2002, 2017), the **caucus laws** of 2010–2016, and the **2024** numbers (60 reserved seats, 306 women candidates, 11 winners, 9.94 million fewer women voters). Every answer ends with a clear **view** and **reforms**: direct election, a stronger candidate quota in winnable seats, and party democracy.
:::

